# HOWTO

These notes assume commands are run from the repository root and Docker Compose
v2 is available as `docker compose`.

## Frontend live local development

Use this workflow when changing the Next.js frontend and you want hot reload
against the local demo backend services.

1. Stop any regular demo stack first so port `8080` is free.

   ```sh
   make stop
   ```

2. Start a one-off frontend development container with the frontend source and
   protobuf definitions mounted from the working tree.

   ```sh
   docker compose --env-file .env --env-file .env.override \
     run --rm -p 8080:8080 \
     -e NODE_ENV=development \
     --volume "$(pwd)/src/frontend:/app" \
     --volume "$(pwd)/pb:/app/pb" \
     --user node \
     --entrypoint sh \
     frontend
   ```

   Compose starts the frontend service dependencies automatically. This command
   runs the frontend service directly, without the `frontend-proxy` container,
   and publishes the Next.js development server at <http://localhost:8080/>.

3. Inside the container shell, install dependencies if `node_modules` is not
   already present or if `src/frontend/package-lock.json` changed.

   ```sh
   npm ci
   ```

4. Start the frontend in live development mode.

   ```sh
   npm run dev
   ```

5. Edit files under `src/frontend` on the host. The running Next.js process in
   the container should reload as files change.

6. Stop live development with `Ctrl-C`, then exit the shell.

   ```sh
   exit
   ```

7. Remove the backend services started for the dev session.

   ```sh
   make stop
   ```

Notes:

- The source bind mount hides the image's installed dependencies. Running
  `npm ci` inside the container creates Linux-compatible dependencies under
  `src/frontend/node_modules`.
- If you normally install frontend dependencies on the host, avoid reusing a
  host-created `node_modules` directory inside the Linux container.
- If you need a smaller backend footprint while doing UI work, use the command
  above as-is. It uses `compose.yaml`, which contains the core services the
  frontend needs.

## Start and stop the full demo

Start the default full demo, including the observability stack:

```sh
make start
```

Open these URLs after startup:

- Webstore: <http://localhost:8080/>
- Jaeger: <http://localhost:8080/jaeger/ui>
- Grafana: <http://localhost:8080/grafana/>
- Load Generator: <http://localhost:8080/loadgen/>
- Feature Flags UI: <http://localhost:8080/feature/>
- Telemetry docs: <http://localhost:8080/telemetry/>

Stop the demo and remove its volumes:

```sh
make stop
```

Other useful start modes:

```sh
make start-minimal
make start-no-o11y
make start-minimal-no-o11y
```

## Rebuild the frontend image

Use this when you want the formal container image path rather than the live
development server.

Build only the frontend image:

```sh
make build service=frontend
```

If the demo stack is already running and you want to rebuild and restart just
the frontend service:

```sh
make redeploy service=frontend
```

If the stack is not running, build the image and then start the demo:

```sh
make build service=frontend
make start
```

The frontend image is built from `src/frontend/Dockerfile` and tagged through
the Compose image settings as `${IMAGE_NAME}:${DEMO_VERSION}-frontend`.
